Presidential elections were held in [[Benin]] on 6 March 2016, having been delayed by one week due to logistical constraints.<ref>{{cite news |date=2016-02-12 |title=Bénin: présidentielle reportée au 6 mars |url=http://www.bbc.com/afrique/region/2016/02/160212_benin |language=fr |website=BBC}}</ref> Incumbent president [[Thomas Boni Yayi]] was at the end of his second presidential term and was constitutionally barred from running for a third. The elections grabbed the interest of many of the country's top businessmen, resulting in over 30 candidates trying to run for the presidency.<ref>{{Cite web
| url = http://www.africanews.com/2016/02/11/benin-s-campaign-trail-gets-busier-ahead-of-polls/| title = Benin’s campaign trail gets busier ahead of polls| website = Africa News | date = 11 February 2016 | access-date = 2016-02-25}}</ref> A second round was held on 20 March,<ref>{{Cite web| url = https://www.voanews.com/a/record-33-candidates-vying-for-benin-presidency/3217961.html| title = Record 33 Candidates Vying for Benin Presidency 
| website = VOA | access-date = 2016-03-05}}</ref> in which businessman [[Patrice Talon]] defeated Prime Minister [[Lionel Zinsou]].

==Background==
During 2015 and 2016 the leaders of some African countries undertook efforts to remain in power after decades of rule; a [[2015 Rwandan constitutional referendum|referendum in Rwanda]] resulted in [[Paul Kagame]] being allowed to remain in power, there was [[Burundian unrest (2015–present)|unrest in Burundi]] over President [[Pierre Nkurunziza]]'s plans to run for a third term, whilst the long-term presidents of Uganda and the [[Republic of the Congo]] ran for office again. However, in West Africa, the long-term president of [[Burkina Faso]] was removed from office and Nigerian President [[Goodluck Jonathan]] left office after losing an election.<ref name=Bloomberg>[https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-03-03/motorcycle-riders-at-forefront-of-benin-s-presidential-campaign Motorcycle Riders at Forefront of Benin's Election Campaign] Bloomberg News, 3 March 2016</ref>

The body charged with producing the new voter cards failed to live up to their deadlines of distributing cards.<ref>{{Cite web
| url = http://www.africanews.com/2016/02/05/benin-delays-in-voter-cards-distribution/| title = Benin delays in voter cards distribution ahead of February 28 polls
| website = Africa News | date = 5 February 2016 | access-date = 2016-02-25}}</ref> This caused the Constitutional Court to first delay the elections by a week, and then make the ruling that old voter cards issued prior to the [[2011 Beninese presidential election|2011 elections]] would also be accepted as the court feared that voters might be disenfranchised.<ref name=":0" />

==Campaign==
Campaigning was only permitted for a 15-day period directly before the elections. The Constitutional Court sanctioned a campaign period from 19 February to 4 March 2016.<ref>{{Cite news
| url = http://www.shanghaidaily.com/article/article_xinhua.aspx?id=320583 | title = Benin's presidential election campaign to start Friday | date = 18 February 2016 | access-date = 25 February 2016 |website=Shanghai Daily}}</ref>

===Candidates===
The [[Résoatao Party]] selected its candidate via party primaries held on 7 November 2015; its leader and sole MP [[Mohamed Atao Hinnouho]] was chosen with 141 votes, with the president of the party's women's branch Eliane Saizonou receiving 14 votes and the party's first councillor Félix Tohoyessou eight.<ref name=AB>[http://www.actubenin.com/?Elections-primaires-du-Parti-ResoAtao-l-honorable-Mohamed-Atao-Hinnouho-designe "Elections primaires du Parti RésoAtao"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304063139/http://www.actubenin.com/?Elections-primaires-du-Parti-ResoAtao-l-honorable-Mohamed-Atao-Hinnouho-designe |date=2016-03-04 }}, Actu Benin</ref>

Prime Minister [[Lionel Zinsou]] announced on 1 December 2015 that he would stand as the candidate of the [[Cowry Forces for an Emerging Benin]], the party of President [[Yayi Boni]], in the 2016 presidential election. He said that he would focus on financing agriculture and helping informal workers obtain formal employment.<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-benin-election-idUSKBN0TL25620151202#Sw0d51bgcJXdb3DT.97 "Benin PM Zinsou confirms to run for president in 2016 polls"], Reuters, 2 December 2015.</ref> Zinsou's French background attracted some criticism. At a meeting of a grouping of Zinsou's opponents held on 5 January 2016, a trade union leader denounced Zinsou as "a colonizer" who was "parachuted in ... to safeguard the economic crimes of Boni Yayi". The government defended Zinsou, stressing that he was "a full citizen of Benin" and that arguing that his opponents were appealing to "base instincts of hatred, racism, fear and intolerance."<ref>Allegresse Sasse, [https://news.yahoo.com/benin-politicians-attack-presidents-favored-successor-212300569.html "Benin politicians attack president's favored successor"], Reuters, 5 January 2016.</ref>

The [[Democratic Renewal Party (Benin)|Democratic Renewal Party]], the third party in parliament led by [[Adrien Houngbédji]], announced on 30 January 2016 that it would support Lionel Zinsou.<ref>Allegresse Sasse, [https://www.reuters.com/article/us-benin-politics-idUSKCN0V80UE "Benin prime minister gets opposition support for presidential election"], Reuters, 30 January 2016.</ref> Independent candidates included the businessmen Patrice Talon and Sebastien Adjavon.

The [[Autonomous National Electoral Commission]] (CENA) announced on 13 January 2016 that 48 people had filed the necessary paperwork to stand as presidential candidates, although their candidacies still needed to be approved by the Constitutional Court.<ref>[https://news.yahoo.com/record-48-candidates-seek-benin-presidency-100958543.html "Record 48 candidates seek Benin presidency"], Agence France-Presse, 13 January 2016.</ref> Each candidate had to submit health certificates that prove a clean bill of health and deposit a filing fee of an equivalent $25,000. The court cleared 36 candidates and dropped 11 candidates for not providing sufficient evidence for various standard requirements. Three further candidates dropped from the race prior to the allocated campaigning period.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|title = Benin: Presidential election campaign begins|url = http://www.graphic.com.gh/international/africa/58801-benin-presidential-election-campaign-begins.html|location=Accra, Ghana|newspaper= [[Daily Graphic (Ghana)|Daily Graphic]]|access-date = 2016-02-24|first = Kwamena|last = Nyamekye}}</ref>

==Results==
Provisional results released on 8 March 2016 showed Lionel Zinsou placing first with 28.4% of the vote. Patrice Talon placed second with 24.8% and Sebastien Ajavon placed third with 23.03%. Zinsou was therefore expected to face Talon in a second round of voting.<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-benin-election-idUSKCN0WA0XN "Benin PM Zinsou faces run-off in presidential election"], Reuters, 8 March 2016.</ref>

For the second round, Talon received the support of 24 out of 32 of the defeated first round candidates. Most notably, he received the backing of Ajavon. The second round was held on 20 March 2016. Saying that the results "point to a decisive victory for Patrice Talon", Zinsou quickly conceded defeat and congratulated Talon.<ref>[https://news.yahoo.com/pm-zinsou-concedes-defeat-talon-benin-president-vote-030547985.html "PM Zinsou concedes defeat to Talon in Benin president vote"], Agence France-Presse, 20 March 2016.</ref>

The Constitutional Court validated the results on 25 March.<ref name=Newly>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-benin-election-idUSKCN0WS08X "Newly-elected Benin president aims to reduce presidential terms"], Reuters, 26 March 2016.</ref>

